\f1\fs22 This time, Haloren Smythe struck perfectly with his three-sided stainless-steel spike, the 
\f2\i Slice of Life, 
\f1\i0 and was rewarded with a fine pulsing spray of oxygen-rich blood. He threw his head back and revelled in it imparadising his chest and chin, and soaking his hair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When his victim finally went limp from insanguination, and Halo regained his composure, he rose and went about his meticulous routine for cleaning up. The routine was calming, a time for reflection as he came back to his more mundane self, a period during which he could truly savour his deeds in a way which was impossible during the furious commission of them. He felt refreshed and, in a way, delivered safe beyond the danger and chaos of his appetites. He\'92d survived and thrived.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Except he was thriving less with every attack. Later in a bar, he found himself already surveying potential marks, and fantasising about his next conquest. The interval between strikes was shortening and the pleasure of each killing was duller. Except for this day\'92s. Perhaps it was the way she offered more resistance than usual, or the increased danger (the flatmate was due back, \'91any minute\'92 now). Halo sobered even as he sipped his beer. He 
\f2\i could 
\f1\i0 have been caught, but he didn\'92t care, and he was rewarded with an experience so pure, it rivalled the early days of chaotic frenzy before he honed and perfected his art.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But he was already thirsty, so soon after his last conquest. He sipped his beer, leaned back in the booth and breathed relaxation into his thumping arteries. He scanned the bar for new targets. None baited his interest, and he knew why. In the darkest caverns of his heart, there stood but a single idol worthy of his calcified attention.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah. She was the ultimate prize. To Halo, she was magnetic. Perhaps he might even stop after her. What a sweet relief that would be.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo pulled out his tablet and tapped open the Labrador app, selected Sarah\'92s profile compiled at the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0  in the precious few seconds his tablet had to sample her breath, pheromones and other key indicators. Sarah was so far off the charts, Labrador assessed her age against various benchmarks as 8, 12, 19 and 28. One benchmark\'97clearly confused by the extraordinary data points\'97put Sarah\'92s age at 226.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Whatever her age, Sarah was a singularly worthy conquest, both for her organic perfection and her awareness of his tech advantages and augmentations. She\'92d be a challenge indeed. Halo placed his tablet screen-down on the table and savoured the possibilities. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All the broadcast screens in the bar were playing his favourite show, 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0 , a semi-documentary series that told little homilies of what happens to Cloudborne hopefuls in \'93business\'94. Spoiler alert: they always get crushed\'97just for fun, of course\'97under the single-origin alpaca-wool sock of some Airborne corporate foot. The episode depicted a hilarious and ultimately doomed family cleaning business which, after winning the largest contract of their lives, mysteriously drowned in a mine tailings pond. 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0  was the second most popular broadcast show in the country and was closing fast on 
\f2\i Candid Criminal
\f1\i0 , a show which Halo hoped would end with its host, Alexander Koalemos, suffering the last trebuchet slap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The dialogue of the show muted. \'91We interrupt normal broadcasting for an address from the CEO,\'92 a new journalist-sounding voice said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo snarled as the flat and slightly alien face of Piotr Dunhilton, the recently appointed Chief Executive Officer of the Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia appeared on all the screens. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No one foresaw the ascendency of Dunhilton after the early retirement of the the previous CEO, even the operatives who facilitated the retirement. Dunhilton was proof-positive that, privilege and corruption would always triumph over competence in Ulyssian politics\'97which should surprised no one. Ulyssia was no-liability sovereign state incorporated in Panama, whose registered address was a recycling wheely-bin on Kangaroo Island.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Less than a year before becoming CEO\'97as Minister of Defence, Border Walls, and Trade\'97he alienated France by cancelling a multi-billion-dollar trade agreement for French toiletries, and instead, formed an alliance with the British and Americans. After much fanfare, it emerged that neither trade partner could supply Ulyssia with any toiletries for the next four decades. Unperturbed, Dunhilton declared the Ulyssian government would fund a soap factory in Adelaide. Detractors pointed out the water the factory required would come from the Murray River, which was already more suds than H
\fs14 \sub 2
\fs22 \nosupersub O. And so the good Airborne meritocrats of Ulyssia found themselves scrubbing their arses with domestic Cloudborne soap, which was widely considered to be composed of cement, bleached coral and sandpaper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My fellow Airborne Ulyssians,\'92 the potato face said into the press conference mic. \'91When I became CEO of our proud profit-seeking country, I promised decent people everywhere I would be a scourge of all lazy undeserving minority groups. I also promised I would strike out against the immoral who expect an even break simply because they exist. I will always fight for true Ulyssians who have virtuously inherited, cleverly grifted, or have been righteously awarded their wealth.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The CEO paused for effect. Halo ground his teeth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91As I am sure you are aware, Cloudborne agitators have escalated their criminal activities in recent times. You have seen the destruction of property in bomb attacks; and the unlawful violence against government agents who work tirelessly for your security. You may have heard of not-for-profit terrorists who conduct their seditious business, poisoning the simple minds of the once peaceful and compliant, and who are now fomenting disunity in their ghettos and enclaves. Yes, these are troubling times, my fellow Airborne, times when those who are intrinsically disentitled agitate for more than they deserve; when those who are worth nothing concoct presumptuous notions of self-worth; when those without connections seek to connect above their stations. Well, let me assure you, I\'92m making it my sacred mission to end all that. I\'92ll hand you over to the new Commissioner of Ulyssia Police Incorporated.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A frog-mouthed fluff-head with perpetual stubble stepped to the mic. Shuffling in behind him, to bask before the camera\'92s eye, was the preposterous Lieutenant Alexander Koalemos. 
\f2\i Lieutenant is not even a real rank in the police force
\f1\i0 , Halo bitterly thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91After intense investigation and extraordinary collaboration between various agencies, we\'92ve been able to plot connections between the recent nefarious activities: the bombing at the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 ; the civil unrest among the Elvi; industrial espionage against Ulyssia\'92s premier fridge company; and the radicalisation of the public transport. Enquiries reveal a single individual is present or connected with all of these incidents. Yesterday morning, we apprehended this individual and we are assisting him with our enquiries.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Commissioner! Commissioner! Can you identify the perpetrator?\'92 came the chorused response from the attending slant of reporters.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Commissioner turned and looked at Alexander who nodded like a gameshow host.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, I can,\'92 the Commissioner said to his audience. \'91The perpetrator is a small-business pretender and probable unionist, named Sukrit Anthony Supaporn.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo almost spat a mouthful of beer at the nearest screen. \'91Tony?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Commissioner!\'92 one of the journos interjected. \'91Can you confirm Cloudborne Supaporn is being investigated for the disappearance of an Airborne Cyber Specialist named Sarah Jacobson?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That made Halo sit up and stare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Commissioner checked with Alexander before answering, \'91Yes, we believe, the perpetrator uses cleaning agents as a means of some form of mind control. We have many reports of this Sarah Jacobson behaving erratically, and we can confirm she was last seen in the presence of the perpetrator.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another journo interjected. \'91Isn\'92t mind control one of the elements in the Nightclub Vampire serial killings?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander shook his head and crossed his arms for the Commissioner, who then said, \'91We know the Nightclub Vampire struck on the night of the bombing of the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 , so it\'92s reasonable to fix our perpetrator up for those crimes as well.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo egested a roar of outrage, snarled, and threw his tablet at a screen, which woke up Labrador from background processing. Whether by bug or by a strange evolution of artificial prescience, it assessed the Commissioner as dead already. .
